			<div class="content" id="post-content-2475309">
									

<p>I found this comment pretty interesting because it goes to something I thought through a lot before writing. And now get to talk about it!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I found it hard to believe that a hardened MTF member, made to recount the most traumatic event of his life, would have the wherewithal to do so in such a politically-correct manner.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sure, this makes sense &#8212; except you're missing that the nameless, genderless narrator (hehe) isn't being politically correct (or even 'respectful', for that matter, since usually in this case it's more an issue of respecting another person than being PC). If they were being politically correct, the pronouns would've been dropped, I agree. The narrator is just <em>used</em> to calling Agent Jann Ekua by the correct pronouns, and used to using correct pronouns in general because of Sigma-3 training to blend in with Hand types.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">More&nbsp;in-depth&nbsp;comments</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Speaking from experience &#8212; both I and my real-life live-in partner are non-binary and use alternate pronouns &#8212; that's very difficult at first (even if you <em>are</em> non-binary it's not like you magically automatically use correct pronouns with no difficulty) and then gets easier after a few months of practice. (Still fuck it up in contexts which you're not used to, but that's another story.)</p>
<p>Also: Sigma-3 is the team most likely to be 'ahead of the curve' on this, because they have to deal with people outside the Foundation and 'blend in' with them, and refusing to use correct pronouns due to, say, a military background or traditional beliefs or whatnot, is a good way to fuck yourself over real fast.</p>
<p>(The Hand seem the GOI by far most likely to use alternative pronouns, at least in some factions; ironically they also contain members who would probably hate that more than anyone! This is based on real-life experience with pagan groups&#8230;. some of them are very open-minded and cool about things, others obsess about male &amp; female energy and get mad about non-binary/trans people.)</p>
<p>Put another way: they are literally trained to use correct pronouns, because it's part of their cover. In our narrator's case, it's become second nature because the training has worked. It's not so different from 'always speaking in another language if you're trained to'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If you really have your heart set on gender-less pronouns, though, might I suggest they/them instead of ze/zir?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I usually choose ze/zir over they/them because then you don't get singular/plural confusion. Plus, in sci-fi and fantasy works in particular, singular 'they' can be more difficult &#8212; because you could really have a gestalt intelligence which isn't non-binary but is a 'they', and therefore give the impression that non-binary people are non-human gestalt intelligences.</p>
<p>(Incidentally, that's also why I personally use ze/zir. I don't really care <em>which</em> alternative pronouns people use for me, but ze/zir tends to be easiest.)</p>
<p>I do wonder if it would be interesting to have the narrator comment on that. It would be realistic enough for the interviewer, who might not have Sigma-3 field training, to mess it up, and have the narrator (who hates the interview and therefore the interviewer) correct it. Probably rudely. Unsure about this, though&#8230;</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
</div>
</div>

