<p>Thanks for your thoughts! That was an interesting read.</p>
<p>Having heard your reasoning, I'm satisfied that your use of ze/zir would make sense in-universe; however, it might not be a bad idea to address this briefly somewhere in the text, as you mentioned (something about it being a way to blend in with the Hand).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>isn't being politically correct (or even 'respectful', for that matter, since usually in this case it's more an issue of respecting another person than being PC)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I agree with you here. Actually, re-reading my comment, I'm not even sure why I used &quot;politically correct&quot; in the first place - aside from being, as you pointed out, a poor choice of words, it doesn't even convey what I was trying to say (I think I'm too tired&#8230;). Allow me to rephrase this better: what I meant was that I thought the narrator, being in a distressed state of mind, would have difficulty not mixing up the pronouns while talking (you mention that difficulty yourself further down), and, being rather rude of nature, wouldn't really care enough to make an effort.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the nameless, genderless narrator</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230; Are you <em>kidding</em> me!? How the hell did I miss this? I was sure the text mentioned he was a man, how weird&#8230; (Of course, this makes it even more plausible that the narrator would be familiar with the use of ze/zir&#8230;)</p>

